Satellite imagery plays a vital role in supervising and protecting our planet, yet monitoring the entire Earth daily from space is impractical due to the vastness of our planet and the limitations of current technology. Consequently, detecting early changes in the environment and managing global resources effectively proves challenging. To address this issue, we have developed EarthNets, a comprehensive platform for processing and analyzing satellite images in real-time, capable of handling multiple datasets from various sources and providing accurate predictions at an affordable cost. By utilizing a unique and efficient architecture, EarthNets can process information at a high speed, enabling seamless integration into existing IoT systems. We present a comprehensive study of our platform, showcasing its capabilities and performance in a practical application. Additionally, we highlight three exciting use cases, demonstrating how EarthNets can be employed in predicting deforestation, monitoring ocean health, and tracking urban development. These examples serve as a starting point for a broader range of applications, empowering governments, non-profits, and individuals to make the most of EarthNets for a wide array of critical environmental tasks. With its flexibility and adaptability, EarthNets serves as an essential tool for a broad range of applications, facilitating a global shift towards AI-driven planet monitoring. 

